WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.540
OpenAI just shared hundreds of new math solutions made by an AI that most people have never seen. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, episode 189. The results come from one of OpenAI's strongest in-house models, not the Chatbot you open after school. It worked on open problems, which are math questions real researchers have been stuck on for years. The company placed the findings in a public GitHub repository, a shared digital locker anyone can open and read.

00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:35.300
Next to the write-ups are proofs typed in a language called Lean, so a machine can grade them.

00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:40.800
Lean is a special computer language that tests whether each math step is actually allowed.

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:47.380
Picture your phone's autocomplete growing so sharp it could finish a whole research paper, not just the next word.

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:49.940
That is the closest everyday picture.

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:58.320
This model did not toss out lucky guesses. It produced full proofs, meaning step-by-step arguments that show why an answer is true.

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:04.180
A proof is like a cooking recipe that lists every move, so another person can bake the same

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:05.620
cake and get the same result.

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:08.560
Other mathematicians can inspect that recipe.

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:11.020
Checking software can inspect it too.

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:15.880
Math is the hidden plumbing under science, engineering, and the apps on your phone.

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:21.760
When unsolved problems start to move, physics, medical research, and new technology can move

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:22.480
faster too.

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:24.320
That is the career layer.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:28.300
The tools you might use at work someday sit on this kind of progress.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:33.140
For students, school and research habits may shift sooner than people planned.

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:34.540
You can look yourself.

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:37.740
Search GitHub for these open AI math proofs.

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:39.840
Open one file and just scroll.

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:45.320
Notice how the argument sits beside a lean version that a computer can grade line by line.

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:51.220
The company also listed the computing effort behind every result, like labeling each trophy

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:53.100
with the hours of heavy lifting it took.

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:56.760
A tough game puzzle is the right picture for the method underneath.

00:01:57.340 --> 00:02:01.920
You try one step, watch what happens, then pick the next step from what you just learned.

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:06.920
The math question is that puzzle, except a person might need years to finish it.

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:12.280
First, the model chops the huge question into smaller pieces it already knows how to handle.

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:18.700
That is like turning a giant school project into a list of tiny tasks you can actually finish one by one.

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:24.040
Second, it tries different paths, the way you test hallways and amaze in Minecraft.

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:30.060
Paths that lead somewhere useful get kept, dead ends get dropped, it repeats that hunt

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:32.700
millions of times, and it does it very quickly.

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:37.420
Third, it writes its reasoning in lean, and lean plays referee.

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:41.040
The referee blows the whistle the instant a step is illegal.

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:45.020
If the whistle blows, the model backs up and tries a different move.

00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:50.600
Over many rounds, those surviving moves stack into a whole proof that people and computers

00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:51.640
can both trust.

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:56.160
This one's a bit tricky, but the simple version is a loop, not a magic trick.

00:02:56.600 --> 00:03:00.360
The system is not reciting memorized answers from a giant answer key.

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:06.020
It is exploring possibilities the way a curious student would, only at a scale no person

00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:07.420
could match in one lifetime.

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:11.340
Once you see that loop, the mystery thins out in a good way.

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:15.780
Chop the problem, test a path, listen for the whistle, try again.

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:21.120
A longtime artist and game developer pointed chat GPT at a different kind of mountain.

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:27.560
Chat GPT, the chatbot a lot of you already type into, became an art factory for a strategy

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:28.940
game about airships.

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:33.300
The developer made more than 1,200 pieces of artwork this way.

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:38.240
The AI handled rooms, icons, enemies, backgrounds, and achievements.

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:43.540
doing that by hand would have taken the artist years working alone. The game takes

00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:49.120
cues from a spaceship title called FTL and a steam release is planned soon. One

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:53.660
person suddenly had a whole art department inside a laptop and a year's long

00:03:53.660 --> 00:03:58.380
list turned into finished images in days. If you like games, stories, or just

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:03.320
doodling characters, you can try a tiny version of that factory right now. Go to

00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:07.560
the chat GPT website and describe a character or a scene for a game you are

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:12.200
imagining. Ask it for several variations, then change a few words and generate

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:17.080
again until one feels right. Watch which details in your description actually show

00:04:17.080 --> 00:04:23.680
up and which ones get ignored. That mismatch is the whole craft. Chat GPT also picked

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:28.920
up a new audio habit, though only on paid plans. Upload an audio file and it can

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:33.440
now build a transcript automatically, a written copy of every spoken word.

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:39.660
Voice notes, lectures, or recordings become text you can search without juggling extra apps.

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:45.660
If you record interviews, class notes, or podcast ideas on your phone, that is a lot less

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:46.920
pausing and retyping.

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:50.840
Think of it like a classmate who types the lecture while you keep talking.

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:56.860
You can test the idea for free today, record a short voice memo on your phone, then ask

00:04:56.860 --> 00:05:01.720
any free AI chatbot to summarize what you said and compare how accurate it feels.

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:08.460
Brian Cheskey, who runs Airbnb as its chief executive officer, argues that AI does not make

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:14.180
everyone equally skilled. He says it widens the gap between people who ask great questions and

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:19.700
people who do not, because everyone now has the same tools. It is like handing the whole class

00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:25.660
the same calculator. The person who knows which problem to solve still pulls ahead. He keeps a

00:05:25.660 --> 00:05:30.720
daily notebook of ideas and uses AI to find patterns across thousands of thoughts each month.

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:35.340
That is a giant pile of sticky notes, with a helper sorting the matching colors.

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:41.640
And Thropic, another AI company, is opening its strongest models to more verified security

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:42.200
researchers.

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:47.480
Those researchers do defensive work, spotting holes in computer systems ahead of people

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:48.460
who would exploit them.

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:53.520
The company is also adding options for authorized testing that helps improve safety.

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:54.920
That's it for today.

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:59.680
Remember, every AI expert started exactly where you are right now.

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:03.600
Now, if something we talked about today made you curious, go try it.

00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:05.680
That's literally how learning works.

00:06:06.200 --> 00:06:09.380
Stay curious, keep experimenting, and we'll see you tomorrow.

